@*
    Which schedule somebody is deducted on, shown in a row.

    Only ever drawn for a landlord or a client. A CAL employee is the ordinary case and
    labelling every one of them would put a chip on nearly every row in the application, which
    turns the marking into wallpaper and means nobody reads the ones that matter.

    The filter above the table is how an official asks "which are the landlords". This is how
    they see it without asking, on the row they happen to be looking at.
*@

@if (Category is not BorrowerCategory.Employee)
{
    <MudTooltip Text="@Explanation">
        <MudChip T="string" Size="Size.Small" Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                 Color="@(Category == BorrowerCategory.Client ? Color.Secondary : Color.Tertiary)"
                 Class="@($"ml-2 {Class}")">
            @Category.DisplayName()
        </MudChip>
    </MudTooltip>
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public BorrowerCategory Category { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    private string Explanation => Category switch
    {
        BorrowerCategory.Landlord =>
            "Deducted against the rent CAL pays them. They appear on the landlord schedule, "
            + "which is drawn on the main account and reconciled separately from the payroll one.",

        _ => "Not a member. They hold no shares and borrow on different terms.",
    };
}
